WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.869
Model selection begins with a job statement, not a leaderboard.

00:00:04.869 --> 00:00:11.008
Describe the input, required output, latency, volume, privacy boundary, error cost, and reviewer.

00:00:11.008 --> 00:00:17.571
A model cannot be judged useful until success is tied to an operational task.

00:00:17.571 --> 00:00:23.710
CCTV event review may need concise descriptions and reliable identification of visible activity.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:29.002
Document and screenshot analysis may depend on small-text OCR and layout.

00:00:29.002 --> 00:00:35.142
Product inspection may require consistent recognition of a narrow defect under controlled lighting.

00:00:35.142 --> 00:00:40.857
Charts and diagrams combine perception with reasoning about labels, relationships, and values.

00:00:40.857 --> 00:00:45.303
Home-lab experimentation may favor broad capability and easy setup.

00:00:45.303 --> 00:00:51.442
Security alerts require conservative output, visible evidence, and human confirmation before consequential action.

00:00:51.442 --> 00:00:57.158
Batch review can tolerate slower inference if throughput and completion are stable.

00:00:57.158 --> 00:01:01.180
Near-real-time monitoring has stricter latency and concurrency needs.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:09.437
A model that answers one image well may not keep pace with several streams or a large queue.

00:01:09.437 --> 00:01:13.882
Define representative normal cases, difficult cases, and costly failures.

00:01:13.882 --> 00:01:17.481
For OCR, specify character accuracy and formatting.

00:01:17.481 --> 00:01:24.044
For descriptions, define which facts must be present and which unsupported claims are unacceptable.

00:01:24.044 --> 00:01:28.489
For counting, state the expected range and occlusion conditions.

00:01:28.489 --> 00:01:33.782
No universal best model exists because use cases value different tradeoffs.

00:01:33.782 --> 00:01:43.308
Shortlist models that fit the task and hardware, then evaluate them locally with the same prompts, runtime conditions, and human-scored criteria.
